# {{lb|en|idiomatic|obsolete|UK|thieves}} A [[Dissenter]]; a [[Protestant]] who is not a follower of the Established [[Church]] (i.e. [[Church of England]], [[Church of Scotland]], etc).
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1876
|editor=Charles Hindley
|publisher=Tinsley Brothers
|location=London
|url=https://archive.org/details/lifeandadventur01greegoog
|title=The Life and Adventures of a Cheap Jack
|page=260
|passage=On one occasion a Jew was selling cocoa-nut when the "'''autem cackler'''," ''i.e.'', dissenting minister, came and wanted to impart to the Israelite the sin he committed in carrying on his vocation on such a day.
}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2014-12-12|author=Sheena Vernon|title=Messiah: Love, Music and Malice at a Time of Handel|publisher=John Hunt Publishing|isbn=9781782797616
|text='Get the '''autem cackler''',' his companion says, pointing at Tyers, mistaking him for a preacher.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2015-01-31|author=Lucia St. Clair Robson|title=Mary's Land|publisher=Lucia St. Clair Robson|isbn=9780990640035
|text="A military cove will damn us first, then tip us a sixpence, whilst an '''autem cackler''' calls down God's blessing and parts not from a thin farthing."}}
